2
00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:05.740
Good news: you don't have to write it all yourself.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.300
Let's learn to reuse it well.

4
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.890
[music]

5
00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:14.860
♪ Most code I need is out there, ♪

6
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.520
♪ Written once and shared. ♪

7
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.720
♪ It's run in working systems, ♪

8
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.560
♪ So bugs were found and repaired. ♪

9
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.500
♪ Less to write and less to test, ♪

10
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.980
♪ So the feature ships sooner. ♪

11
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.040
♪ But modules aren't neat building blocks: ♪

12
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.180
♪ Each one assumes its world, ♪

13
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.730
♪ And glue code joins the pieces ♪

14
00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.100
♪ Where those worlds don't fit. ♪

15
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.040
[music]

16
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.760
♪ Before you reuse, ask: ♪

17
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.260
♪ What will it save? What will it cost? ♪

18
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.020
♪ What does it assume? ♪

19
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.460
♪ What does it pull in? ♪

20
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
♪ Pin it, but update for patches. ♪

21
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.740
♪ External reuse is not a one-time investment. ♪

22
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.060
♪ My code had not changed a line, ♪

23
00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.060
♪ Yet docker broke today. ♪

24
00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.140
♪ Docker calls on Requests; ♪

25
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.400
♪ Requests calls urllib3. ♪

26
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.560
♪ No version had been pinned, ♪

27
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.600
♪ So a clean install got two; ♪

28
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.930
♪ Version two changed its API, ♪

29
00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:10.780
♪ And Requests' calls broke too. ♪

30
00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:14.030
♪ Pin dependency versions: ♪

31
00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:16.880
♪ Nothing moves till you choose. ♪

32
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.160
♪ OpenSSL had a flaw; ♪

33
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.420
♪ For two years, no one saw. ♪

34
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.040
♪ A request claimed a length; ♪

35
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.550
♪ The server trusted it ♪

36
00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:27.840
♪ And sent back its own memory: ♪

37
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.360
♪ Passwords, private keys. ♪

38
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.770
♪ I didn't write that bug, ♪

39
00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.170
♪ But I shipped it, all the same. ♪

40
00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.620
♪ The fix shipped within a week ♪

41
00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:37.830
♪ To all who chose to update; ♪

42
00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.940
♪ That's reuse on your side: ♪

43
00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.120
♪ One fix, shared by all. ♪

44
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.780
♪ Pinned doesn't mean frozen: ♪

45
00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.120
♪ Update to the fix, then test. ♪

46
00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.840
♪ Before you reuse, ask: ♪

47
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.360
♪ What will it save? What will it cost? ♪

48
00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.160
♪ What does it assume? ♪

49
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.560
♪ What does it pull in? ♪

50
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.140
♪ Pin it, but update for patches. ♪

51
00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:02.920
♪ External reuse is not a one-time investment. ♪

52
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:04.980
♪ Left-pad: eleven lines ♪

53
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.120
♪ That pad a string on the left. ♪

54
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.080
♪ Babel and React pulled it in ♪

55
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.760
♪ Through packages they used. ♪

56
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.980
♪ Its author unpublished it; ♪

57
00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.620
♪ Thousands of builds failed. ♪

58
00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:17.600
♪ Many never saw it: ♪

59
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.690
♪ A dependency, deep down. ♪

60
00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:21.770
♪ When eslint-scope was hijacked, ♪

61
00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:23.860
♪ Its new release stole tokens. ♪

62
00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:25.940
♪ Write trivial code yourself; ♪

63
00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.020
♪ Keep dependencies few: ♪

64
00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.760
♪ Each one can break, be hijacked, ♪

65
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.320
♪ Or vanish out of view. ♪

66
00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.280
♪ But code that's hard to get right, ♪

67
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.650
♪ Like crypto: don't write your own. ♪

68
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:39.040
♪ Choose maintained and popular, ♪

69
00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.590
♪ But fit comes before fame. ♪

70
00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.780
♪ Code built for Ariane Four ♪

71
00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.500
♪ Stored horizontal speed ♪

72
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.080
♪ In sixteen bits: enough, ♪

73
00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.480
♪ Since that rocket flew slower. ♪

74
00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.480
♪ Reused in Ariane Five, ♪

75
00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.660
♪ Which flew faster: overflow! ♪

76
00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.840
♪ Thirty-seven seconds in, ♪

77
00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.740
♪ It veered, then self-destructed. ♪

78
00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.110
♪ It worked where it was built, ♪

79
00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:02.120
♪ And failed where it was sent: ♪

80
00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.840
♪ Its assumption didn't hold. ♪

81
00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.840
♪ Identify violated assumptions: ♪

82
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.460
♪ Read the docs and read the code, ♪

83
00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:09.920
♪ Check what it was built for, ♪

84
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.130
♪ Then test it where it goes. ♪

85
00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.360
♪ If two systems drift apart, ♪

86
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.520
♪ Version it, or clone and own: ♪

87
00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.360
♪ Copy it, so each can change. ♪

88
00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:20.940
♪ Library or framework? ♪

89
00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.500
♪ Ask who calls whom. ♪

90
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.200
♪ I call axios when I choose; ♪

91
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.860
♪ Express calls my code. ♪

92
00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.800
♪ That's the Hollywood Principle: ♪

93
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.960
♪ Don't call us, we'll call you. ♪

94
00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.450
♪ It decides more; I write less, ♪

95
00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.380
♪ But it's harder to undo. ♪

96
00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.850
♪ Your turn! Try these cases. ♪

97
00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.900
♪ A package just to check if a number is even? ♪

98
00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.480
♪ (crowd) No. It's one line: write it. ♪

99
00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:47.220
♪ Keep dependencies few, so write your own encryption? ♪

100
00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.420
♪ (crowd) No: reuse the tested one. ♪

101
00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:52.230
♪ Your pinned library gets a security fix? ♪

102
00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.040
♪ (crowd) Update on purpose, then test. ♪

103
00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.460
♪ Your old date code now runs for users in Europe? ♪

104
00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:00.570
♪ (crowd) Check if it assumes month first. ♪

105
00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:04.760
♪ Two apps share a module, and their needs drift apart? ♪

106
00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.520
♪ (crowd) Version it, or clone and own. ♪

107
00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:11.540
♪ pytest runs the tests you wrote: library or framework? ♪

108
00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.020
♪ (crowd) Framework: it calls your code. ♪

109
00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:16.880
♪ Before you reuse, ask: ♪

110
00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.180
♪ What will it save? What will it cost? ♪

111
00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:21.020
♪ What does it assume? ♪

112
00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:23.420
♪ What does it pull in? ♪

113
00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:25.960
♪ Pin it, but update for patches. ♪

114
00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:31.700
♪ External reuse is not a one-time investment. ♪

115
00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:35.280
npm install left-pad?

116
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.780
No need: padStart is built in.

117
00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:43.220
That's reuse done right.

118
00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:50.370
[music]
